course: myths
lesson: 30
title: How would we know? Asking experts and spotting tricks
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some claims you can't test with two seed trays in the kitchen: whether MSG is safe, whether a bridge is sound, whether a photo of a giant squid is real. For those you ask the people whose job is checking.
explain: The magic question is "How would we know?" If a cat had nine lives, what would we count? If a study shows red wins, how big was the difference, and did anyone repeat it?
explain: Watch for tricks: an edited photo of a real animal, a selling word like "superfood", or "everybody knows", which sounds like evidence and isn't.
explain: But checking isn't the same as doubting everything: the vet's poster about grapes and dogs is TRUE, and an expert in the right field is usually worth trusting.
explain: Tricks are made by people who want clicks, sales or a laugh; understanding how they work is how you stop being fooled, never how you fool others.
fact: Cats have one life; they are agile (quick and good at balancing) and often survive falls and scrapes, which is where the saying comes from.
fact: A real study found that Olympic fighters wearing red won slightly more often, but the difference was small, only in some sports, and other studies argue about it; skill and training matter far more.
fact: Giant squid are real deep-sea animals, but photos of them can be edited; a giant squid on a British beach would be reported by real news outlets and museums, and a grown-up can do a reverse image search (asking a search site where else the photo has appeared).
fact: When you cook with alcohol, some of it always stays in the food; long simmering removes most but not all.
fact: Large studies found MSG (a powder added to food to make it taste savoury) safe for most people in normal amounts; tomatoes and cheese have natural glutamate too, and old fears about it were partly mixed with unfair attitudes to Chinese food.
fact: Macs can get viruses and other harmful software; they are just attacked less often than Windows computers.
fact: Old bridges and buildings can weaken over time, so engineers inspect them regularly.
fact: "Superfood" is a selling word, not a science word; blueberries are healthy, but no single food cures diseases.
fact: "Everybody knows" is not evidence; many things everybody knew, like the Sun going round the Earth, were wrong.
fact: Grapes and raisins can cause kidney failure in some dogs (kidneys clean the blood), and vets have seen many cases, so dogs should never be given them.
fact: Some amazing world records are real and are checked by Guinness World Records; other amazing photos are edited.
fact: "How would we know?" is a good first question for any claim.
exercise: How many lives does a cat have? | One | Nine | Seven | It depends on the cat's colour
exercise: Why do people say cats have nine lives? | Cats are agile and often survive falls and scrapes | Cats really do come back to life, up to eight times | Old scientists counted nine hearts in a cat | Cats sleep nine times a day
exercise: What did the study about wearing red find? | A small advantage for red in some sports, still argued about | Red always wins every sport by miles | Red makes you lose every time | Red has no effect on anything, ever
exercise: When someone says "a study shows", what should you ask? | Who was studied, how big the difference was, and did others repeat it | What colour the study was printed in | Whether the study had a famous name | How long the study's title was
exercise: Are giant squid real? | Yes, they live in the deep sea | No, they are only in legends | Only in Cornwall, nowhere else | No, they died out with dinosaurs
exercise: A photo shows a giant squid on a Cornish beach. How could you check it? | A grown-up does a reverse image search and checks real news | Look at how many people have liked it | Zoom in until it looks real | If it's scary, it's real
exercise: Does all the alcohol burn off when you cook with it? | No, some always stays in the food | Yes, every drop burns off in seconds | Yes, as soon as it touches the pan | No, cooking makes even more alcohol
exercise: What do large studies say about MSG? | It is safe for most people in normal amounts | It makes almost everybody feel ill straight away | It is banned in every country | It is only safe in Chinese food
exercise: Which everyday foods have natural glutamate? | Tomatoes and cheese | Apples, pears and plums | Bread and plain water | Rice and a glass of milk
exercise: Can Macs get viruses? | Yes, they are just attacked less often | No, Macs are completely immune to them all | Only if they are switched off for a long time | Only the very oldest ones from years ago
exercise: "If a bridge was going to fall, it would have fallen already." What's true? | Old bridges can weaken, so engineers inspect them | Old bridges get stronger every year | Bridges never need any checking once they are built | Only new bridges can ever fall down
exercise: What is "superfood"? | A selling word, not a science word | A food scientists proved cures diseases | A food grown in space | A food that gives superpowers
exercise: Is "everybody knows it's true" good evidence? | No, many things everybody knew were wrong | Yes, if lots and lots of people keep saying it | Yes, it ends the argument fairly for everyone | Only if your teacher or a parent says it too
exercise: The vet's poster says grapes are poisonous for dogs. Is it right? | Yes, grapes and raisins can harm dogs' kidneys | No, it's just a myth, like cats having nine lives | Only green grapes, not red ones | Only for cats, not for dogs
exercise: Jude sees a photo of a 50-centimetre moustache. How could he check? | Look up Guinness World Records or a news report | Believe it, because photos can't lie | Measure his own dad's moustache | Ask whoever shared it with you if it's really real
exercise: What's a good first question for any claim? | How would we know? | Who is the loudest person saying it? | How many times was it shared? | Is it exciting enough to be true?
test: "my cat fell off the shed and was fine, she's on life 4 now lol" What's true? | Cats are good at landing, but they only have one life | She really has five lives left now | Cats lose one of their lives every single time they fall | Cats can't get hurt from any height
test: Max says, "Red kit = win, a study proved it, so I'll win Saturday." What's the sensible view? | The effect was small and argued about; training matters far more | He's sure to win if he wears red | Red kit makes the other team lose | The study proved blue makes you lose
test: Holly says, "Giant squid are real, so this photo must be real." What's wrong with her reasoning? | A real animal can still be shown in an edited photo | Giant squid aren't real, so it's fake | Photos of real animals can't be edited by anybody at all | It's real only if the squid is moving
test: Gran says the wine in the stew "all burns off". What's fair to say? | Some alcohol always stays, though long cooking removes most | She's right, every bit of it disappears | Stew makes more alcohol the longer it cooks | Alcohol turns into sugar when it's hot
test: Raj's uncle refuses takeaway because of MSG. What does the evidence say? | It's safe for most people, and tomatoes have glutamate too | He's right, MSG makes everyone very ill | MSG is only dangerous in takeaways | Nobody has ever studied MSG
test: "nah Macs can't get viruses mate, my sister said" What's the right correction? | They can, they're just attacked less often | Only Windows computers can ever get a computer virus | Macs get more viruses than any computer | Viruses only attack phones now
test: A smoothie bottle says "SUPERFOOD — fights disease!" What should you think? | It's a selling word; it may be healthy but cures nothing | It must cure illness, it says so on the bottle | It's poison, because it's an advert | It's medicine, so you need a doctor's note
test: Arthur ends every debate with "everybody knows it". What's the best reply? | How does everybody know? Let's check | Then it must be true, you win | Nobody knows anything, ever | Let's vote and the majority is right
test: Elsie's dad thinks the grape warning is "just another myth". What should she say? | This one's true, vets have seen many cases | He's right, it's like nine lives | Grapes are only bad for big dogs | Raisins are fine, it's only fresh grapes that hurt
test: What would happen if no one inspected an old bridge for fifty years? | Weak spots could grow without anyone noticing | It would definitely be stronger than ever before | Nothing, because bridges can't weaken | It would turn into a new bridge
test: Which source is best to check a giant squid photo? | A real news outlet or a museum | A comment under the post | A meme page with lots of followers | A friend who saw it shared
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "superfood"? | A marketing expression lacking scientific definition | A scientific category of foods proven to cure disease | A legal classification of medicinal produce | A term denoting foods cultivated in orbit
exam: Identify the principal reason for which canine animals ought not to be given grapes or raisins. | They may cause failure of the kidneys in some dogs | They are invariably harmless to dogs of every breed | They cause the fur to change colour | They impair the canine sense of smell
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the cooking of food with alcohol. | A proportion of the alcohol invariably remains | The alcohol is entirely eliminated instantaneously | The alcohol content increases with heating | The alcohol is converted wholly into water
exam: It is asserted that "everybody knows" a certain proposition. Determine the evidential value of such an assertion in itself. | It constitutes no evidence of the proposition's truth | It constitutes conclusive proof of the proposition | It renders the proposition legally binding | It proves the proposition false
